    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, `disable` is used to turn off an interface (equivalent to Cisco's `shutdown`), while the absence of `disable` (or `delete interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable`) would enable the interface (equivalent to Cisco's `no shutdown`). However, the exact translation of `no shutdown` in Juniper would be to ensure the interface is not disabled, which is the default state. If you want to explicitly enable it, you could use: \\n\\n`delete interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable` \\n\\nBut the most direct equivalent is simply not disabling it, as interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper. The above answer assumes you want to explicitly enable the interface. If you just need the direct command translation, it would be:\\n\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable\\n##\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, `disable` is used to turn off an interface (equivalent to Cisco's `shutdown`), while the absence of `disable` (or `delete interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable`) would enable the interface (equivalent to Cisco's `no shutdown`). However, the exact translation of `no shutdown` in Juniper would be to ensure the interface is not disabled, which is the default state. If you want to explicitly enable it, you could use: \\n\\n`delete interfaces ge-3/0/1 disable` \\n\\nBut the most direct equivalent is simply not disabling it, as interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper. The above answer assumes you want to explicitly enable the interface. If you just need